%erg 0.1
Title: Claim ticket numbers in tickets/.claims/NNNN so git detects a collision
Created: 2026-10-21
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-10-21T15:17Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context
Asked by the author (2026-10-21), from the IDH harness repo, where the collision had to be policed by a CI job
(`cross-pr-ticket-collision`: fail a pull request whose added ticket ID another open pull request also adds) that
was switched off the same day with GitHub Actions. Evidence of the pain: `docs/erg-manual.md` (ID allocation is
optimistic: two concurrent invocations in different checkouts can take the same number), the existing
post-check retry loop, the 200 ms "branch-tip scan exceeded" warning of `erg new`, and the harness's own CI
guard built for it (git-erg#282). Today two branches that each add `tickets/NNNN-slug-a.erg` and
`tickets/NNNN-slug-b.erg` have different paths, so git sees no conflict; the duplicate surfaces only when the
corpus check (`erg check`) runs on the merged tree.

## Design (the author's)
- `erg new NNNN` also writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN`, a small file whose content is the ticket's slug.
- Two branches claiming the same number create the same path with different content: git reports an add/add
  conflict when the second one is merged or rebased. The later claimant resolves it by renaming to the next
  free number (its ticket file, its claim file, references in its own log), then continues.
- Claims are never deleted when a ticket closes: the directory is the record of numbers ever used.
- Optional: the holes in the claim numbers are free numbers, so `erg new` can allocate from the claims on the
  merged base plus the claims on branch tips, and the lowest hole is a valid answer.

Notes for whoever builds it:
- The content must differ between claimants. An empty file, or the same slug, would merge cleanly. Slug
  plus nothing else is enough; two different tickets with the same slug and the same number are the same
  ticket by any reading.
- Detection happens when the second claimant reaches the base (rebase or merge), not at allocation. Two
  sessions can still pick a number at the same moment. A stronger option exists for a central origin (push
  an atomic ref `refs/erg/id/NNNN`), at the price of needing the network. Not part of this ticket.
- Existing tickets: decided by the author, 2026-10-21: populate the claims on migration. A migration step
  (`erg migrate`, or its successor) writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN` with the slug for every existing ticket,
  open and closed, so the directory is complete from the first day and the holes are real holes. It is
  idempotent; running it twice changes nothing.
- Check the repository's ignore rules: some repos (the IDH harness) ignore everything and allowlist paths;
  `.claims/` must be addable there or the claim silently disappears.
- `erg check` reports a claim without a ticket, a ticket without a claim (after migration), and a claim whose
  slug does not match the ticket file's slug.
- Per `AGENTS.md` § Earning a feature the burden is on adding: the near-misses above are the evidence; if
  collisions stop biting (the gate catches duplicates at the merge candidate), the ticket can be closed unbuilt.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `erg new` writes `tickets/.claims/NNNN` with the slug; `erg close` leaves it in place.
- [ ] A test with two branches adding the same number with different slugs: the merge reports an add/add conflict
      on the claim file (positive control), and a merge of claims with different numbers is clean (negative
      control).
- [ ] `erg new` allocates from claims (base and branch tips) and may reuse a hole; a documented rule.
- [ ] The migration populates the claims for all existing tickets (open and closed) and is idempotent; `erg check` reports claim/ticket mismatches, including a ticket with no claim after migration.
- [ ] The manual's ID-allocation section and `tickets/AGENTS.md` describe the claims and the rename step.
- [ ] `make check` passes; the bootstrap binary is rebuilt by the normal route.
